From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 18:57:16 -0400
Subject: folders: drop a dragged tab on a folder header to move it in

Folders were menu-driven: the only way into one was "Move Tab to New
Folder", which always makes a new folder. Dropping a tab on an existing
folder's header now appends it to that folder, keeps it active, and
highlights the header while the pointer is over it.

The model half is TabStripModel::MoveTabsToFolder over a new
TabStripCollection::MoveTabsIntoFolderRecursive; the loop that walks nodes
into a folder is shared with the new-folder move rather than copied. Tabs
move one by one, so the tree stays valid however the selection overlaps the
folders involved. Pinned tabs are the essentials row and are refused: the
header is not a drop target for one, and the model refuses the move too.

The view half is FolderDragTarget, the shape SpaceDragTarget has, with one
difference: folder headers sit inside the tab strip, which the drag
controller attaches to before it asks anyone else. A three-line hunk in
TabDragController asks the window for a target inside the strip first, and
BrowserView routes that to the vertical strip's folder target. While a
header is hovered the target hands back the attached context, so the tab
stays in the strip instead of popping out into a window.

Detaching a tab clears its own pinned flag, so pinned-ness is read from the
drag session on each update. The folder is held by handle, not pointer: a
folder whose only tab is the one being dragged is peeled out of the tree
mid-drag, and the drop then leaves the tab in the strip rather than
dereferencing a freed folder. Both are unit-tested with a fake controller.

docs/features/folders.md names these as F7, F8 and F9.

Why: folders without drag-into are a gesture short of Arc's, and the
evidence says folders are the gate for switchers.
Removable when: the tab strip's own reorder becomes folder-aware, at which
point a header drop is just a move to the folder's last position.
